This module, besides featuring and revising all the material seen in the previous three modules, further consolidates the acquisition of Maltese as a foreign language. Participating students will extend the knowledge in terms of vocabulary and simple structures in Maltese introduced to them in Module 2 by creating extended short dialogues in both spoken and written form. Particular attention will be given to the application and revision of grammatical structures discussed in the previously outlined Module 3.

Unit 1 – Making an Appointment in Person
Unit 2 – Making an Appointment on the Phone
Unit 3 – Groceries and Daily Necessities
Unit 4 – Getting to One’s Destination
Unit 5 – A Reservation at the Restaurant
Unit 6 – Ordering Food & Drinks
Unit 7 – Making a Health-related Appointment
a)	better pronunciation and intonation;
b)	improved linguistic knowledge and competence;
c)	adaptability to a variety of communicative situations. a)	formulate short yet fluent phrases in Maltese with regard to various topics;
b)	use intonation to transform statements into questions;
c)	apply notions acquired from previous modules for a natural expression in Maltese;
d)	distinguish words and phrases with similar but not identical meanings;
e)	make correct usage of pronunciation guidelines introduced in earlier modules for a better comprehension;
f)	structure dialogues using common discourse devices. a)	most common daily expressions in Maltese;
b)	typical Maltese interjections and their usages;
c)	elision and contractions used in Maltese speech;
d)	basic conjunctions and other combining elements in Maltese;
e)	filler phrases used by native Maltese speakers to enhance naturalness.

This course does not lead to a particular occupation. However, it provides the necessary nutritional background to support decision-making in enhancing fitness. In particular, targeted individuals are athletes, sports coaches, fitness instructors, and others who want to make a conscious effort to improve their health and well-being. 

It may also add to the knowledge background of those reading qualifications in Health and Social Care, and Hospitality, and who have a desire to help their clients improve their dietary habits.
The learner will be able to: 

a) name the main macro and micro nutrients needed for healthy bodily functions;
b) identify the nutrients and associated food sources required to enhance the differing components of fitness;
c) define digestion, absorption and assimilation;
d) label the human digestive system;
e) match the parts of the digestive system to their respective function;
f) name legal supplements and ergogenic aids commonly used by athletes in enhancing fitness and performance;
g) name national and international agencies responsible for anti-doping;
h) list the components of food labels;
i) write a food diary;
j) understand the role of the nutritionist. At the end of this programme, the learner will be able to:

a) design meals to enhance different components of fitness;
b) practice competition nutrition strategies;
c) use knowledge of the human digestive system in decisions regarding nutrition;
d) plan appropriate volumes of water intake to fit a particular training session/competitive event;
e) demonstrate knowledge of artificial supplements and other ergogenic aids;
f) show awareness of the agencies and associated policies responsible for anti-doping procedures;
g) use the information on food labels to assemble meals accoding to certain calorific and nutritional requirements;
h) show awareness of the concept of motivation and its role in maintaining high levels of sports fitness.

This course is aimed at providing the learner with foundational knowledge, skills and competences required to work as assistant tile layers. Through this training programme, the learner will be able to follow health and safety procedures and will achieve basic skills in laying floor and wall tiles, both for internal areas and outside areas surrounding buildings under supervision.
a)	Describe health and safety procedures at the workplace erect and dismantle a system scaffold and assess equipment up to 2m high;
b)	Sequence the safe use of tools and associated safety precautions;
c)	Identify one’s responsibilities in executing work practices safely;   
d)	Describe how the various hazardous materials on a work site are correctly identified and handled in accordance with health and safety procedures;
e)	Identify the first aid facilities that exist within the work area;
f)	Describe how tools, personal protective equipment and clothing are used in accordance with health and safety regulations and manufacturer recommendations;
g)	Describe the use of various system scaffold platforms and access equipment for less than 2m high;
h)	Identify the designated personnel responsible for dealing with emergency situations;
i)	Identify various clamps and accessories available on the market designed for temporary security fences/ rails installation;
j)	Identify the basic axis of symmetry;
k)	Describe how vertical and horizontal datum lines are used;
l)	Describe how levelling instruments, straight edges and measuring instruments are used for small and large rooms;
m)	Identify floor and wall tiles from given specifications such as ceramic, concrete and marble; a)	Apply health and safety procedures at the workplace including when erecting and dismantling a system scaffold and access equipment up to 2m high;   
b)	Demonstrate the safe use of tools and associated safety precautions;
c)	Plan how to execute work practices safely;
d)	Demonstrate how to identify and assess hazards at the work place and report risks to designated personnel;
e)	Show how various hazardous materials on a work site are identified and handled in line with health and safety procedures;
f)	Use tools, personal protective equipment and clothing in line with health and safety regulations;
g)	Practice undertaking tasks in a manner that is safe to oneself and to other personnel;
h)	Choose suitable tools for a given task and take appropriate safety precautions;
i)	Examine how the various hazardous materials on a work site are correctly identified and handled in accordance health and safety procedures;
j)	Examine tools, personal protective equipment and clothing for visual defects in accordance with health and safety employer procedures;
k)	Propose how to perform tasks in a manner that is safe to oneself and to other persons;
l)	Express instructions to crane operators through non verbal communication;
